WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.880
Hey, you Two Cents viewers!

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.211
Here with a special announcement: Make sure
you make it to the end of the video where

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.360
we get to share some exciting news!

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.300
You're pregnant again?

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.700
Ha ha ha ha...

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.100
The United States has the world’s largest
economy and our workers have some of the highest

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:17.419
salaries in the world.

00:00:17.419 --> 00:00:21.560
But if you think that most Americans have
piles of money, great credit, and low amounts

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.400
of debt, you’d be wrong!

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.429
Since you’re following our channel, it’s
probably obvious to you that our financial

00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:33.260
literacy is at crisis levels, but have you
ever stopped to wonder what led us to this point?

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.580
And more importantly…what can be done to
change it?

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.680
In 2015, the S&amp;P Global Financial Literacy
Survey asked 4 simple financial questions

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.820
to respondents all over the world, and the
results landed the U.S. in 14th place for

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:56.920
financial know-how.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.220
And it shows.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.480
Americans are failing the grade by nearly
every metric.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.680
Nearly 60% of U.S. households don’t keep
a budget.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:10.799
Two thirds would be unable to scrounge up
$1,000 for an emergency and 35% have a debt

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:12.759
currently in collections.

00:01:12.759 --> 00:01:17.620
The average college student carries $30,000
in student loans, and floats a credit card

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.100
balance of around $8,000.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:27.040
The median retirement savings for Americans
age 55-64 is around $100,000 - an amount that

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.120
would translate to just $310/mo of income
in retirement.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:37.890
And it’s especially scary for our generation
— even though 69% of millennials rate their

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:43.900
own financial knowledge highly, only 24% are
able to demonstrate “basic financial literacy”.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.040
One of the biggest factors is the lack of
any formal financial lessons in schools.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.039
Even though the Council for Economic Education
found that kids who receive financial education

00:01:54.039 --> 00:01:58.859
are more likely to save, more likely to pay
off their credit cards each month, and less

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.480
likely to become compulsive buyers, only a
third of states currently offer it!

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.680
And of those that do, most of them incorporate
the subject inside of another course of study,

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.160
like economics, math, or social studies.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.060
Only five states require a semester-long course
focused on personal finance.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.560
But the problems don’t stop there.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:22.820
The minute high-schoolers graduate, they’ve
got a target on their backs and the financial

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.240
industry is lying in wait.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:29.830
Credit card companies are notorious for luring
kids into high-interest credit cards with

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:34.430
offers of T-shirts or a free pizza - despite
the fact that most students have little to

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:37.730
no income and hardly any real-world financial
experience.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:42.720
Unfortunately, that trend of financial institutions
targeting the non-savvy doesn’t let up as

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.000
you get older.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.480
Current rules in the US allow brokers (under
the guise of “financial advisors”) to

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:53.240
put their own financial interests ahead of
consumers when selling a financial product.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:54.480
For real!

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.380
To be fair, the financial industry does engage
in some education.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.430
But consider this…In 2013, the Consumer
Financial Protection Bureau reported that

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:09.110
while $670 million dollars was spent by the
industry on consumer education, $17 billion

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:11.110
dollars was spent on marketing their products.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.320
That’s a ratio of 25 to 1.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.780
Chump change.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:20.750
Make no mistake, the cumulative results of
people not saving enough or getting into financial

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.870
trouble doesn’t just fall on them and their
families, but on society as a whole!

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.700
So what can we do to help promote change?

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:33.720
Consistently scoring at the top of such surveys
are the Scandinavian countries like Denmark,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:34.910
Norway and Sweden.

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:36.940
There are a lot of reasons for this.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:41.350
In Sweden’s case, for instance, there is
a long cultural tradition of saving.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:45.150
No major disasters in the financial markets
to undermine trust in the system.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:50.370
And the presence of the Financial Supervisory
Authority, a strong government agency designed

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.430
to regulate the entire financial services
industry.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:59.200
But probably most important is that kids are
taught about the basics of personal finances

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.480
starting in the first grade and continuing
all the way up to high school.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.540
Changes have to be made to education here
in America if we’re to stand a chance of

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.550
improving things.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:15.260
And you don’t have to be a parent to advocate
for a child’s right to quality financial education.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:20.420
You can look at this map to see what standards
for financial literacy your state currently

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.430
mandates.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.939
You can also reach out to your state representative
to let them know what you’d like to see

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.820
offered to future generations.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.900
Unfortunately, the primary reason cited for
not including this kind of education in schools

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.200
is that it’s the parents job.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.660
But we all know that’s not really happening.

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:41.280
Nearly half of Americans don’t talk with
their children about money at all.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.800
And it’s not because they don’t think
they should!

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.949
It’s because most simply don’t know what
to say.

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:52.039
If you’re a parent, or hope to be one someday,
you do not have to be an expert to teach kids

00:04:52.039 --> 00:04:53.270
about money.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.750
Simply getting them their own bank account
seems to have positive results with 15-year-olds

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:02.360
scoring 40 points higher in financial literacy
than those without one.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:07.960
Kids also learn best by doing, so next time
you sit down to pay your bills online, call

00:05:07.969 --> 00:05:11.509
your kid over and coach them through how to
do it themselves!

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:16.540
For bonus points, check out our previous video
about how to talk to kids about money!

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:21.840
This is so important because if finances aren’t
talked about much growing up, we tend to continue

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.810
that approach of avoidance even when we leave
home.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.550
We just stumble along assuming everyone knows
more than we do, afraid to call attention

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:30.710
to our ignorance.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:36.370
And it’s that unspoken taboo around the
topic of money that allows this negative feedback

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:37.830
loop to continue.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:43.240
Thankfully, it’s never been easier to take
your financial education into your own hands!

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:49.030
The last decade has seen a tsunami of blogs,
podcasts, programs, books and courses aimed

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:52.960
at filling in the adult education gap when
it comes to finance.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.249
And there are tons of amazing FREE or low-cost
resources out there.

00:05:57.249 --> 00:06:01.330
Hundreds of libraries all over the country
host “Money Smart Week's where they cover

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:06.169
topics like retirement, budgeting, and credit
— without any incentive to make you a paying

00:06:06.169 --> 00:06:07.169
customer!

00:06:07.169 --> 00:06:11.749
There’s also the National Endowment of Financial
Education’s website smartaboutmoney.org

00:06:11.749 --> 00:06:13.590
with lots of free courses.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:16.900
You can even ask your employer to bring in
a financial educator!

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:20.970
After all, a better educated workforce is
in everyone’s interests.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:26.449
As a dedicated watcher of two cents, we hope
you continue to broaden your own financial

00:06:26.449 --> 00:06:27.449
education.

00:06:27.449 --> 00:06:30.569
And don’t keep the lessons you’re learning
all to yourself!

00:06:30.569 --> 00:06:35.030
We expect you to share your newly earned money
smarts with those you care about.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:38.889
Changing the face of financial literacy here
in the states and around the world is not

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:41.240
just our job, it’s yours too!

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.240
And that’s our two cents!

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.840
The vast majority of financial education out there is funded by big corporations.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.240
We, and our co-creators, chose to partner with PBS specifically because we believe financial

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.840
education should be free from "for-profit" hidden agendas.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:02.080
And over the last year it's been made abundantly clear you guys want that too!

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.600
We've gained hundreds of thousands of subscribers, scooped up a bunch of awards and are blessed

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.600
with a community of enthusiastic people willing to share their stories and experiences.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:17.720
But did you know that each episode takes between 40 and 50 hours of work to make?

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:24.080
Yea, these amazing visuals and carefully written scripts are being made by a team of full-time professionals

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.640
so I think you can tell where we're going here....

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.560
We're starting a Patreon!

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.680
Now, before you groan we of all people know how important your hard earned dollars are

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:39.120
and we have given our perks a lot of thought to make it worth your while.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.720
That's right! We're going to be inviting you backstage for an intimate look behind the scenes,

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:49.120
a new space to chat together as a community and even the chance to digitally hang out with us

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.680
on a monthly basis.

